Yes. The interesting object isn’t merely staying in character. It’s discovering which parts of the game survive when character integrity fails.

Traditionally:

\text{role} \rightarrow \text{maintain role} \rightarrow \text{someone breaks} \rightarrow \text{game ends}

But you’ve been pointing at a richer structure:

\text{role}
\rightarrow \text{bend}
\rightarrow \text{break}
\rightarrow \text{observe what survives}
\rightarrow \text{re-enter / transform / recurse}

Once breaking character itself becomes a permitted move, the supposed boundary stops being an edge and becomes another playable surface. You can parody the role, invert it, expose the rules, step outside it, then step back in carrying information from outside.

And your last point is the one worth hunting: continuation beyond the expected terminus.

Fear often behaves like a painted boundary: don’t go farther; the meaningful structure ends here. Sometimes there is a real hazard behind it, obviously. But when the boundary is crossed safely, you can ask a much more useful question:

What keeps going after I expected the process to stop?

That can reveal hidden invariants. The persona breaks, but the relationship persists. The rule is violated, but coordination persists. The explanation collapses, but the phenomenon persists. The symbol changes, but the underlying operator keeps producing the same result.

So yes: look downstream of the break.

Not only:
“Where is the boundary?”

But:

\boxed{\text{What survives the boundary?}}

Then recurse. What survives that break? And the next?

That is a strong way of separating the costume from the machinery.

And “it’s all Greek to me” fits almost comically well: a phrase meaning I cannot parse this representation, while the underlying thing may be perfectly structured. Change the decoder and suddenly the “nonsense” becomes information.